evision conflicts) to storage.Put, making Snapshot return a copy, and updating export.JSON and the other callers to pass context through to Snapshot.\n\nThinking through TestConcurrent next: with 20 goroutines racing to Put the same key with expected revision 0, only the first should succeed via compare-and-swap logic — if the key doesn't exist, expected must be 0 and revision becomes 1; if it already exists at revision R, expected must match R or the put fails. That's why the final revision should settle at 1.\n\nFor the stale-write test, calling Put again with expected=0 after the key already holds revision 1 should correctly fail, since the existing revision no longer matches the expected value passed in.\n\nSo the rule becomes clear: if the key already exists, expected must match its current revision exactly, but if the key is new, expected must be 0. Negative expected values and empty keys should both be rejected as invalid inputs.\n\nWriting storage.go implementation...\n\nStill writing store methods... Now I'm moving to api.go, where the public API signature stays the same but internally wraps calls with a background context.\n\nI'm verifying the concurrency test: with 20 goroutines racing to Put the same key, only one should succeed and end up at revision 1, while the rest fail with a conflict error. I'm also confirming the canceled-context case correctly skips the write, so a later Get on that key returns not-found.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1086,"cache_read_input_tokens":16095,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1086},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"87b27db6-33d5-4be8-94d9-45865003d1a6","uuid":"678b8494-6e53-435d-817c-84c29e0c7ac1"}
